What a fresh Composery instance looks like before anyone configures it.

Every hunk here is a first-run default, expressed in upstream's own state so a
user's own choice simply replaces it and then persists:

- Views hidden until opted into (outline, timeline, npm, scm repositories,
  recommended extensions) and containers left off their bar (run and debug,
  output, debug console, plus the accounts entry). A hidden view is
  hideByDefault and an absent container is merely unpinned, so the bar's context
  menu puts either back, and nothing is re-evaluated against the id lists once
  the user has seen the container. Container LOCATIONS are deliberately
  untouched: moving scm/markers/extensions/terminal between bars is a layout
  opinion rather than a default, and it lived in per-browser storage where it
  could not have survived a second device anyway.
- Welcome: the Coder promo block becomes the "Set up an AI coding agent" card
  grid (composery.installAgent, via the composery-agents builtin), keeping the
  getting-started override gate. Welcome slides scroll natively on touch - never
  Gesture-target them, because a registered target preventDefault()s touchend
  and kills every plain click in the page.
- Watermark: workspace tips reduced to commands/goto/recent.
- Extensions view: the default "Popular" list becomes "Themes"
  (category:themes @popular) - themes are the one kind of extension every fresh
  instance wants.
- Menubar: web-navigation actions belong to the File root, not to every nested
  submenu as well.
- Terminal: 4px top and bottom padding (TerminalInstance._getDimension subtracts
  computed padding, so this reserves real rows rather than clipping them), and
  the inline view action hidden by default.
- The insecure-context notice can be dismissed for good.

